# A value that comes from a header and is written nowhere in this file
# (design.md §4.19, panel 038).
#
# Every number printed below is `math.h`'s or `stdio.h`'s. Nothing here can be
# copied wrong, because nothing here is copied: the emitter reads the name where
# the preprocessor can see it, and clang checks its type against the real header
# with the same `_Static_assert` an `extern function` gets.
#
# Two headers on purpose. The `#include` walk used to ask `FnKind` whether a
# declaration came from a header — and an `extern constant` lowers to
# `FnKind::Constant`, not `FnKind::Extern`, so a group holding *only* constants
# would have had its `#include` silently dropped. `stdio.h` here holds no
# function, which is the shape that catches it.
#
# It is also the case that makes the double-emit determinism test non-vacuous for
# this form: that test walks `tests/golden/emit` and `tests/golden/run` only
# (`golden.rs`), so before this file the accessor and its two assertions were
# emitted by nothing the test could see (panel 038, compiler-engineer's condition).
#
# **AND A FIXED DEFECT, named after it** (Go's `test/fixedbugs`), 2026-08-13 —
# whose symptom is a **platform** rather than a program, which is why it is
# written here instead of in a case of its own.
#
# SYMPTOM: this file passed on Darwin and failed on Linux, on the first CI run
# this project ever had, with `error[ffi_unknown_name]: `math.h` declares no
# `M_PI``. The compiler was right both times.
#
# CAUSE: `M_PI` is **not in ISO C** — it is POSIX/XSI — and glibc guards it
# behind `__USE_MISC`, which `-std=c11` switches off by defining
# `__STRICT_ANSI__`. Darwin's `math.h` does not guard it at all. So the flags,
# not the types, decided whether a correct binding compiled.
#
# FIX: `-std=gnu11` (panel 047). The two dialects differ by exactly one
# predefined macro, and on glibc that macro's only effect is to hide `M_PI`,
# `strdup`, `fileno`, `setenv` and nine others — most of what §1.11 says a
# program binds.
#
# **Do not delete the `M_PI` binding as gratuitous.** It is the only line in the
# repository that fails when the dialect is wrong: everything else the corpus
# binds — `sqrt`, `pow`, `floor`, `puts`, `getenv`, `abs`, `SEEK_SET` — is ISO C
# and compiles under either. Measured: 63 of 63 `run/` goldens cross-compile for
# `x86_64-linux-gnu` under `gnu11`, and 62 of 63 under `c11`. This is the one.

extern "math.h" link "m"
    constant M_PI: f64
    function sqrt(x: f64) -> f64

extern "stdio.h"
    constant SEEK_SET: i64
    constant SEEK_END: i64

function main()
    # `3.14159…` is in `math.h`, and the program says only its name.
    print(M_PI)
    print(sqrt(M_PI * M_PI))
    # Two `i64` macros, and the arithmetic is the language's own.
    print(SEEK_SET)
    print(SEEK_END - SEEK_SET)
